==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|wind'ard}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|wind|ward}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɪndwə(ɹ)d/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-windward.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɪnə(ɹ)d/|a=nautical}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-windward2.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Towards the wind, or the direction from which the wind is blowing.
#: {{ux|en|The '''windward''' boat must give way to the leeward boat.}}
# On the side exposed to the wind.
#: {{ux|en|the '''windward''' bedroom}}
# {{lb|en|metaphoric}} In a position of [[advantage]] or [[superiority]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1872| author=| title=Publications of the Narragansett Club: George Fox digg'd out out of his burrowes|page=257| passage={{...}} but while they own what ''G. Fox'' hath written, and that he writ it with a perfect spirit: I say untill they do make some ''Recantation'' or ''Retractation'': or shew the Reasons why they doe not, ''H. Norton'' who keeps more plainly to his ''Principles'' is to '''windward''' of them, and the ''Foxians'' do but strip themselves naked to be more derided and scorned as the more notorious ''Juglers'' and ''Dissemblers'' .}}

